.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt RESTORE_MINIXFS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm restore_minixfs
.Nd restore a dump into a MINIX file system
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl t
.Op Fl c
.Fl f Ar file
.Nm
.Fl r
.Op Fl cNv
.Op Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
.Op Fl W Ar bits
.Op Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
.Op Fl s Ar symtable
.Fl f Ar file
.Ar image
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads BSD dumps in either byte order and restores them into a MINIX
file system of any version; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
Supported dumps include:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Xr dump_minixfs 8 ;
.It
.Nx
and
.Fx
UFS1 and UFS2 dumps;
.It
Linux ext2, ext3 and ext4 dumps;
.It
4.2BSD and 4.3BSD dumps, including older file system formats.
.El
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl t
List the names of the files in the dump, each after its inode number in
the dump, as
.Xr restore 8
does.
The dates, the level and the label of the dump come first.
.It Fl r
Restore the dump into the root directory of
.Ar image :
a full dump into an empty file system, then each dump of a higher level
in the order they were made.
Each directory of the dump comes to hold what the dump says it holds:
names that are gone are removed and new ones made, a file whose last
name went away is freed, and files and directories take their modes,
owners and times from the dump.
.It Fl c
Read the directories as those of V7, of 16-byte entries, as
.Xr restore 8
does with
.Fl c .
Those of a dump of a file system older than 4.2BSD are always read so.
.It Fl f Ar file
The dump;
.Ql -
is standard input.
.It Fl M Ar size : Ns Ar heads : Ns Ar side
Write to an image containing the file system on one disk side.
See
.Xr minixfs 1
for the track layout.
.It Fl W Ar bits
Take the bit maps of a big-endian file system as words of
.Ar bits
bits, 8, 16, 32 or 64, instead of 16 for V1 and V2 with names of 14 and
8 for the rest; see
.Xr minixfs 5 .
.It Fl N
Check everything, and write nothing.
.It Fl o Ar uid : Ns Ar gid
Make every file owned by
.Ar uid
and
.Ar gid ,
numbers both.
.It Fl s Ar symtable
Record the dump-to-image inode mapping and the restored dump's date in
.Ar symtable
for subsequent incremental restores.
The default is
.Pa restoresymtable .
.It Fl v
Name each directory and file as it is restored.
.El
.Pp
Before writing,
.Fl r
checks name lengths and the number of available inodes.
If either check fails, nothing is written.
When reading a file, it also checks owners, device numbers and file sizes
in advance; any error prevents writing.
When reading standard input, it skips files that exceed these limits,
including their names, and continues restoring the rest.
Device numbers use major \(mu 256 + minor and must be below 65536.
.Pp
An incremental dump's previous-dump date must match the date in
.Ar symtable .
Keep this table between restores.
.Pp
The destination must be marked clean.
It is locked with
.Xr fcntl 2
while it is written, and one that another program holds is refused.
The clean flag is cleared during writing and restored after the data
reaches disk.
Failures due to space or file system limits, or a truncated or damaged
dump, leave the written data consistent.
After correcting the cause, retry the same dump.
.Pp
An I/O error or forced termination may leave an inconsistent image with
the clean flag cleared.
Run
.Nm fsck_minixfs Fl y
before further writes.
If the image or restore table cannot be recovered, restart from the full dump.
.Pp
Extended attributes, file flags and sockets have no place in a MINIX
file system, and are left out with a warning.
So are the names of files that the dump does not hold, as in a dump of
a part of a file system.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits with 0 on success, 1 if anything failed, and 2 for a usage error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Restore a full dump and one of level 1 made after it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# newfs_minixfs -V 3 -s 65536 usr.img
# restore_minixfs -r -f /backup/usr.0 usr.img
# restore_minixfs -r -f /backup/usr.1 usr.img
# rm restoresymtable
.Ed
.Pp
Put a dump of an ext2 file system of Linux into a V2 file system, owned
by root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# restore_minixfs -r -o 0:0 -f home.dump home.img
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr minixfs 1 ,
.Xr minixfs 5 ,
.Xr dump 8 ,
.Xr dump_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr fsck_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr newfs_minixfs 8 ,
.Xr restore 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Shoichi Fukusaka
.Sh BUGS
Compressed dumps of Linux and dumps on more than one volume are not
read.
